unsafe module;

// hashing.tr — a general-purpose hash(T), STD-003 item 6
// (docs/planning/ARCHITECTURE_OVERHAUL.md §8). Exists to give Map<K, V>/
// Set<T> (map.tr/set.tr) a real O(1)-average hash index instead of their
// old O(n) linear scan -- previously impossible ("there is no generic
// hash(T) in the language yet", map.tr's own former header comment) for
// the identical reason the Map<string, V> equality gap
// (docs/planning/LIMITATIONS.md item 1) used to exist: a generic method
// body was type-checked and codegenned exactly ONCE, shared across every
// instantiation, so a call like `hash(key)` inside it would have seen
// `key` as the STATIC, erased `TurfGenericParamType("K")` -- no concrete
// overload could ever match. Monomorphization (TYPE-002) gives every
// instantiation's method body its own re-typecheck with K/T concrete,
// which is what makes `hash(key)` dispatch correctly now, verified live
// the same way `self.keys[i] == key` already does (map.tr's own
// Map.indexOf() doc comment).
//
// `unsafe module;` stays (STD-003 item 8 considered and rejected here too
// -- see list.tr's own note for the full reasoning): `fn<T> hash(x: T):
// int`'s own parameter is `T`, genuinely unconstrained -- a caller can
// legally reach this with `T` substituted to a raw pointer type (e.g. a
// `Map<SomeStruct*, V>`'s own internal `hashing::hash(key)` call), and the
// SUBSTITUTED parameter (`x: SomeStruct*`) then needs this file's raw-
// pointer capability regardless of the scoped `unsafe { }` already wrapped
// around this function's body -- confirmed live via `Map<Point*, int>`.
//
// Design: exactly two overloads.
//   - `hash(x: string): int` -- a real content hash (djb2), since string's
//     runtime representation is a POINTER to bytes: hashing the pointer
//     itself would hash by identity, not content, which is wrong the same
//     way a raw `==` on two unrelated string allocations used to be.
//   - `fn<T> hash(x: T): int` -- a generic fallback: a byte-wise hash
//     (also djb2) over the `sizeof(T)` bytes at `x`'s own address. This
//     covers every OTHER type with no per-type code at all: int, int32,
//     double, bool, char, any pointer type, and any plain-data struct.
// Ordinary (non-operator) overload resolution prefers an exact concrete
// candidate over a generic one -- verified live -- so `hash("a")` reaches
// the string overload and `hash(42)`/`hash(someStruct)` fall through to
// the generic byte-hash. No trait/constraint system needed or added
// (ARCHITECTURE_OVERHAUL.md §25 item 1 forbids one) -- overload
// resolution alone is enough because every concrete K/T actually used
// anywhere in this codebase today is exactly `int` or `string`.
//
// Correctness requirement any hash(T) must satisfy: `a == b` implies
// `hash(a) == hash(b)`. The byte-hash meets this for every plain-data
// type, since it is a pure function of the same bytes `==` itself would
// compare structurally field-by-field for such a type. It does NOT, in
// general, meet this for a struct whose own `operator==` compares two
// POINTER-shaped fields (e.g. two strings) by content while their raw
// bytes (the pointers themselves) differ -- see this group's own change
// record, *Known pitfalls*, for the full explanation and why it's an
// accepted, documented limitation rather than something fixed here.
//
// Overflow note: every step below is masked to 32 bits with `& 0xFFFFFFFF`
// specifically because FREEZE.md §6.2 traps runtime signed-integer
// overflow -- a classic unmasked djb2 (`h = h * 33 + byte`) panics after
// only a handful of iterations under this compiler's checked arithmetic,
// confirmed live while developing this file. Masking keeps `h` bounded
// below 2^32 before every multiply, so `h * 33` never exceeds ~2^37 --
// nowhere near int's 2^63 range.

import strings;

fn hash(x: string): int {
  mut h: int = 5381;
  immutable n: int = strings::strlen(x);
  mut i: int = 0;
  while i < n {
    // FDN-004: `x[i]` is `char`; widen to `int` before the arithmetic (no
    // implicit char<->numeric conversion, FREEZE.md §6.2 row 4).
    h = (h * 33 + (x[i] to int)) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return h;
}

fn<T> hash(x: T): int {
  mut h: int = 5381;
  unsafe {
    immutable p: char* = &x to char*;
    immutable n: int = sizeof(T);
    mut i: int = 0;
    while i < n {
      h = (h * 33 + (p[i] to int)) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
      i = i + 1;
    }
  }
  return h;
}
